        "message": {
          "role": "assistant",
          "content": "Every Saturday morning, a woman named Mara opened a tiny repair shop beside the train station. Its window displayed no new products, only rescued ones: a lamp with a crooked shade, a radio missing its dial, and a red bicycle whose bell rang like a spoon against a thin glass.\n\nPeople arrived carrying things they had almost thrown away. Mara listened before reaching for her tools. A cracked teapot had belonged to someone\u2019s grandfather. A stuffed fox needed one ear reattached before a child could sleep. Sometimes the repair was simple; sometimes it was impossible, but listening never was wasted.\n\nOne rainy afternoon, a boy brought in a clock that had stopped at six seventeen. He asked whether she could make time move again. Mara smiled and said she could try to make the hands move. The difference mattered, though neither of them could explain why it mattered so much.\n\nWhile she worked, trains came and went. The boy watched passengers hurry beneath umbrellas, each convinced of being late for something. Inside the shop, minutes took a different shape. They became screws laid in careful rows, dust brushed from gears, and the quiet pleasure of paying close attention to everything.\n\nAt dusk, the clock ticked. The boy carried it home wrapped in newspaper, holding it against his chest. Mara switched off the shop lights. Across the street, the station clock announced another departure. She stayed a moment longer, listening to two imperfect rhythms keeping one small corner of darkness company.",
